    description: "WHAT. dead-code-maybe's call-evidence rule (PR #2326, cli.call_evidence_groups) asks one thing of a (language, kind) group: does at least one call edge land on a member. One edge clears the whole group, so an analyzer that resolves one call in a thousand is treated as seeing callers, and every other unreached callable of the group is reported dead. Seen on the measured maps only from the other side: mastodon's 7 JavaScript methods have no incoming call edge and 5 are withheld; a repository with 7,000 methods and one resolved method call would have none withheld.\n\nALSO OPEN. By grep, 12 of 120 analyzer modules mention a function or method kind and never the string calls (proto, qml, rst, scss, thrift, capnp, gnuplot, twig, cmake, make, play_routes, sql); only sql, cmake and scss were confirmed by a run. The other nine are unconfirmed.\n\nASK. Measure, per language and kind on a corpus, the share of callables with an incoming call edge, and decide whether the rule needs a second tier (a group whose share is far below the fleet's is labelled, not cleared). Confirm the nine by a run.\n\nFound during PR #2326 (2026-11-22). Evidence: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/three_pr_plan_11222026/pr1/RESULTS.md section 3."  # 4ebf
